WEBVTT

00:00:07.388 --> 00:00:12.537 
In the previous tutorial, we explored the basics of WoT forms and bindings.

00:00:12.537 --> 00:00:19.168 
 Now that we understand their structure, let's see how protocol bindings work in practice.

00:00:19.168 --> 00:00:25.214 
When you want to write a Consumer application, you don't need any prior knowledge about the protocol being used.

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:32.783 
Instead, the Consumer reads the Thing Description, looks at the available forms, and selects one it understands.

00:00:32.783 --> 00:00:37.767 
 Let's walk through four protocols using our smart coffee machine example.

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:43.593 
For each one, we'll trace exactly how a WoT operation relates to real network traffic.

00:00:43.593 --> 00:00:53.699 
 First, we want the Consumer to perform the " readProperty" operation on "coffeeBeansLeft" so we can get the remaining

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:59.306 
 To achieve this, the Consumer reads the form and translates it into a network request.

00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:04.145 
The " href" tells it the address and that HTTP is the protocol.

00:01:04.145 --> 00:01:11.802 
Then the "htv:methodName" field says to use a " GET" request and the "contentType" tells it to expect a JSON

00:01:11.802 --> 00:01:12.712 
in return.

00:01:12.712 --> 00:01:15.490 
So the Consumer sends the following request:

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.876 
 The Thing receives this, reads its internal sensor, and responds:

00:01:20.876 --> 00:01:29.699 
 The Consumer parses the JSON body and now has the "coffeeBeansLeft" value of 320 grams. Notice how each field

00:01:29.699 --> 00:01:35.714 
in the form had a direct counterpart in the request — the WoT form is essentially a declarative description of

00:01:35.714 --> 00:01:37.560 
the HTTP request.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.687 
 Now the Consumer wants to invoke the "brewCoffee" action.

00:01:41.687 --> 00:01:48.068 
 The Consumer reads this form just like before — but now " href" starts with " coap", so it

00:01:48.068 --> 00:01:50.825 
knows to use CoAP instead of HTTP.

00:01:50.825 --> 00:01:54.548 
The "cov:method" field says "POST".

00:01:54.548 --> 00:02:01.978 
The contentType is "application cbor", so this time the Consumer encodes the action input for a medium sized coffee in

00:02:01.978 --> 00:02:02.920 
CBOR.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.469 
 The resulting CoAP message looks something like this:

00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:14.774 
 The Thing receives the message and sends back a CoAP response with code "2.04 Changed", confirming the action was

00:02:14.774 --> 00:02:15.591 
accepted.

00:02:15.591 --> 00:02:23.507 
From the Consumer's perspective, this was identical to invoking the HTTP action — it performed an " invokeaction" operation and

00:02:23.507 --> 00:02:25.124 
got a success response.

00:02:25.124 --> 00:02:32.781 
 The fact that it used CoAP instead of HTTP, or CBOR instead of JSON, was determined entirely by what

00:02:32.781 --> 00:02:34.101 
was written in the form.

00:02:34.101 --> 00:02:38.654 
 For the "lowOnWater" event, the form looks like this:

00:02:38.654 --> 00:02:45.079 
The " op" field is " subscribeevent", which tells the Consumer it needs to listen rather than request.

00:02:45.079 --> 00:02:53.361 
The href points to an MQTT broker and includes the topic "lowOnWater" — that the
 Consumer should subscribe to.

00:02:53.361 --> 00:02:58.712 
 Now the Consumer is registered and waiting.

00:02:58.712 --> 00:03:04.800 
On the Thing side, when the water level drops below the threshold, it publishes to the same topic:

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.118 
 The broker forwards this to the Consumer.

00:03:08.118 --> 00:03:16.520 
 The Consumer receives the message, parses the text as declared by "contentType", and now has the event data: 50

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:17.935 
milliliters remaining.

00:03:17.935 --> 00:03:25.337 
What's important here is that the WoT operation " subscribeevent" naturally maps to MQTT's publish subscribe model.

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:32.334 
The Consumer didn't need to know it was MQTT — it simply performed a " subscribeevent" operation, and the form

00:03:32.334 --> 00:03:34.521 
told it exactly how to do that:

00:03:34.521 --> 00:03:39.774 
connect to this broker, subscribe to this topic and expect a plain text response.

00:03:39.774 --> 00:03:46.075 
 Modbus is a traditional industrial protocol widely used in automation and control systems.

00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:54.520 
While it predates modern web technologies, WoT protocol bindings make it possible to expose Modbus-based devices through a standardized Thing

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.387 
Description.

00:03:55.387 --> 00:04:03.179 
This allows legacy industrial equipment to be integrated into modern web-based systems, without changing the underlying protocol.

00:04:03.179 --> 00:04:08.502 
 Let's trace how the Consumer translates this form into a Modbus message.

00:04:08.502 --> 00:04:13.130 
The "base" URI gives it the device address and port 502.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:18.462 
The 1 at the end is the Unit ID — the Modbus device identifier on the bus.

00:04:18.462 --> 00:04:23.260 
The " href" value "10003" is the register address.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:30.646 
The " modv:function" field says "HoldingRegister", which can be read or written to, and are typically used to store sensor

00:04:30.646 --> 00:04:32.562 
values like a water level.

00:04:32.562 --> 00:04:39.776 
The "contentType" is set to "application octet-stream", which means the expected response is a sequence of bytes.

00:04:39.776 --> 00:04:43.386 
Putting this all together, the Consumer sends its request.

00:04:43.386 --> 00:04:52.371 
 The Consumer receives those two bytes and interprets them as a 16-bit big-endian integer. Because the Thing Description specifies

00:04:52.371 --> 00:05:00.698 
that "waterLevel" is an integer, the WoT runtime automatically turns this into the number 300— the water level in milliliters.

00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:04.435 
 This is WoT's value fully on display.

00:05:04.435 --> 00:05:12.286 
A Consumer that only knows about " readproperty" operations and Thing Descriptions, just read a binary Modbus register, without needing

00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:17.221 
to know anything about function codes, register addresses, or byte encoding.

00:05:17.221 --> 00:05:25.344 
All of that knowledge lived in the form, expressed through "modv:function", " href", and the base URI.

00:05:25.344 --> 00:05:30.147 
The form is the bridge between the abstracted WoT operations and the protocol.

00:05:31.981 --> 00:05:33.399 
To summarize:

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:37.500 
- Bindings define how interactions are executed over the network

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.153 
- They are expressed using "forms" in the Thing Description

00:05:41.153 --> 00:05:44.582 
- Different protocols fit different interaction patterns

00:05:44.582 --> 00:05:48.910 
- WoT unifies them under a single, consistent interaction model

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:54.894 
 You can proceed to the next tutorial by clicking on the card or the video description.

